<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">While Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown promising progress in general multimodal tasks, they often struggle in industrial anomaly detection and reasoning, particularly in delivering interpretable explanations and generalizing to unseen categories. This limitation stems from the inherently domain-specific nature of anomaly detection, which hinders the applicability of existing VLMs in industrial scenarios that require precise, structured, and context-aware analysis. To address these challenges, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAGE</span>, a VLM-based framework that enhances anomaly reasoning through Self-Guided Fact Enhancement (SFE) and Entropy-aware Direct Preference Optimization (E-DPO). SFE integrates domain-specific knowledge into visual reasoning via fact extraction and fusion, while E-DPO aligns model outputs with expert preferences using entropy-aware optimization. Additionally, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AD-PL</span>, a preference-optimized dataset tailored for industrial anomaly reasoning, consisting of 28,415 question-answering instances with expert-ranked responses. To evaluate anomaly reasoning models, we develop Multiscale Logical Evaluation (<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MLE</span>), a quantitative framework analyzing model logic and consistency. SAGE demonstrates superior performance on industrial anomaly datasets under zero-shot and one-shot settings. The code, model and dataset are available at <a href="https://github.com/amoreZgx1n/SAGE" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/amoreZgx1n/SAGE</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Anomaly Detection, Vision-Language Models, Preference Optimization
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.07939v1/figure1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/284;" width="476" height="284" alt="The begin figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">a. Comparison between our SAGE and GPT-4o. SAGE identifies the uncommon anomaly and provides a correct answer. b. An illustration of SAGE: a VLM using fact enhancement and preference optimization with comparison learning.</span><span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">The begin figure</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Industrial visual anomaly detection plays a crucial role in ensuring product quality, operational safety, and cost efficiency in manufacturing and maintenance systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Early approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Defard et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reiss et al., 2021</a>)</cite> typically formulate anomaly detection as an unsupervised classification or segmentation problem, aiming to determine whether an anomaly exists and localize the anomalous regions. Existing methods can be broadly categorized into three types: Embedding-based methods (<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, SVDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tax and Duin, 2004</a>)</cite>, PatchCore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roth et al., 2022</a>)</cite>) extract feature embeddings using pre-trained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and learn boundaries for normal samples to detect deviations; Data augmentation-based methods (<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, CutPaste <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, DRAEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ezeme et al., 2019</a>)</cite>) synthesize pseudo-anomalies through various data transformation strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cimpoi et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pérez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, enabling supervised learning for anomaly detection; Reconstruction-based methods (<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, MemAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, RD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng and Li, 2022</a>)</cite>) adopt a compression–reconstruction paradigm, learning the patterns of normal samples and identifying anomalies based on reconstruction error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, with the advancement of vision-language models, methods such as AnomalyCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and WinCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2023</a>)</cite> leverage CLIP’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> zero-shot or few-shot capability to extract aligned embeddings for images and text descriptions, exploring the use of image–text pairing with anomalous keywords. Beyond CLIP, Vision-Language Models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, with larger architectures and enhanced multimodal reasoning capabilities, are also being investigated for anomaly detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Although these methods have achieved remarkable progress in identifying known anomalies within curated categories, they often rely heavily on large quantities of normal samples for each specific class. As a result, they typically lack the ability to provide interpretable reasoning and to generalize to unseen categories in long-tail industrial environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world industrial scenarios, anomaly detection often requires deeper reasoning capabilities to answer what (<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, anomaly types), why (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, root causes), and how (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, severity and downstream implications). For instance, detecting a defective component on an assembly line may not only involve localization but also diagnosing the potential cause, prioritizing corrective actions, and anticipating its impacts on subsequent processes. Advanced VLMs offer a preliminary form of anomaly reasoning through their interactive dialogue capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, their performance is often constrained by the diverse and large-scale web-based pretraining data, which may conflict with the domain-specific definitions of normality required in industrial contexts. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ SAGE: A Visual Language Model for Anomaly Detection via Fact Enhancement and Entropy-aware Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), a capsule is incorrectly classified as normal by GPT-4o, as the model relies on generic commonsense patterns (<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, detecting cracks or spots), while failing to recognize domain-specific anomalies (<span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, color inconsistencies) that are critical in industrial quality control. In contrast, human experts can quickly learn the characteristics of normal samples by leveraging prior experience and commonsense reasoning. These limitations highlight the need for advanced frameworks that effectively integrate visual evidence with domain-specific knowledge, enabling interpretable, generalizable, and actionable anomaly detection frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we aim to identify anomalies through <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison-based Learning</span>, where the model analyzes the test image in relation to a given normal reference image that provides patterns of normality and contextual cues. Most existing VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, pre-trained primarily on single-image inputs, these models struggle to accurately interpret multi-image comparisons. To mitigate this, we design specific prompts that first guide the model to attend to the reference image in order to establish an expected baseline, thereby enabling more accurate reasoning about potential semantic deviations in the test image. Considering that VLMs lack the domain experience of human experts, we further incorporate preference learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and fact enhancement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which leverage expert rankings of different reasoning outcomes to align the model’s responses with domain-specific criteria. As a result, the model can more effectively interpret reference images and generates higher-quality responses, aligned with expert expectations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose a VLM-based framework for anomaly detection and reasoning, termed SAGE, which fine-tunes a pretrained VLM model with two novel strategies: Self-Guided Fact Enhancement (SFE) and Entropy-aware DPO (E-DPO) alignment. Specifically, unlike existing VLM-based methods that directly identify anomalies based on a single test image, our approach adopts a comparison-based input paradigm, where a test image is paired with a reference image sampled from normal classes. During the SFE, we first extract fact information (<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, key characteristics and conditions) from the test image leveraging pretrained VLM, which is guided by a customized prompt template designed to summarize image content. These facts are then fused with the visual features of both input images via a cross-attention mechanism, which is subsequently fed into the LLM to perform anomaly reasoning.
By using the E-DPO, the VLM is further tuned to align its outputs with expert preference distributions. This is achieved via entropy-aware direct preference optimization, which leverages the entropy differences across multiple ranked response candidates generated for each input image pair. To support this, we further construct a new multiple-choice dataset, <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AD-PL</span>, comprising 28,415 preference-based QA instances. Each instance includes a test–reference image pair along with four candidate answers ranked by domain experts. To comprehensively evaluate the quality of anomaly reasoning responses, we propose Multiscale Logical Evaluation (MLE), which leverages GPT-4o to quantitatively measure performance across three key dimensions: identification accuracy, localization precision, and reasoning consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAGE</span>, a VLM-based framework for anomaly detection and reasoning. SAGE employs comparison-based learning by utilizing a normal image as a reference to enable deep anomaly reasoning on a given test image.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design two fine-tuning strategies: SFE, which incorporates domain-specific knowledge for visual reasoning, and E-DPO, which aligns model responses with expert preferences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a high-quality dataset with 28,415 QA instances, each with four ranked candidate answers for industrial anomaly reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a comprehensive quantitative evaluation framework for anomaly reasoning models. Our method exhibits superior performance under both zero-shot and one-shot evaluation settings on the MANTA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> and MPDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jezek et al., [n. d.]</a>)</cite> datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.07939v1/main.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/273;" width="476" height="273" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The architecture of our proposed SAGE. It consists of two training strategies: Self-Guided Fact Enhancement (SFE) and Entropy-aware Direct Preference Optimization (E-DPO). SFE fine-tunes the VLM (including the adapter, fusion attention layer, and LLM) based on comparison-based learning, in which the feature differences between the test and reference images are fused with extracted fact sentences using a cross-attention mechanism. E-DPO utilizes multiple preference-ranked answers to select response pairs with the largest entropy difference among candidates; during this stage, the LLM and adapter are fine-tuned accordingly.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anomaly Detection.</span> Conventional anomaly detection (AD) methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf et al., 1999</a>)</cite> typically learn dataset-specific normal patterns via paradigms like one-class classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen and Hoshen, 2020</a>)</cite>, reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng and Li, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, data augmentations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, feature embedding comparison <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bergmann et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. More recently, pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs), especially CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, have gained attention due to their generalization capabilities. These VLM-based approaches adopt various strategies, including comparing image embeddings against textual descriptions of “normal” and “abnormal” in the CLIP embedding space (<span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, WinCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>), often enhanced via attention mechanisms or window-based comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, adapting VLMs for AD tasks via fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024c</a>)</cite> or prompt learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and leveraging foundation models such as Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirillov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for text-prompted anomaly
localization. Some methods, such as AnomalyGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> further integrate Vicuna <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to facilitate reasoning, localization, and interpretability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the above methods, SAGE adopts a comparison-based paradigm by utilizing paired test–reference images, where the reference image represents normal patterns. This design not only improves anomaly localization accuracy but also provides explicit normal references, making it particularly well-suited for domain-specific industrial applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision-Language Models.</span> Recent advancements in VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alayrac et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> have significantly enhanced multimodal understanding by integrating powerful vision encoders with Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhery et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Foundational models such as CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, pre-trained on large-scale image–text pairs, exhibit strong zero-shot generalization capabilities and have influenced a wide range of downstream tasks. The development of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs), such as GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and open-source alternatives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, further enhances reasoning and pushes the boundaries of visual–textual reasoning and interaction. These models have been applied to various tasks, including detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, video analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnab et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and visual reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alayrac et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Their capabilities are continually improved through techniques such as contrastive prompt learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, supported by large-scale resources like LAION <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and OpenCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilharco et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, most existing VLMs are not inherently designed for anomaly detection and often lack the ability to perform comparison-based reasoning. In contrast, our method fine-tunes VLMs to anomaly domains by integrating domain-specific commonsense while also aligning model reasoning with expert preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SAGE framework is designed to enhance the anomaly reasoning capabilities of the VLM, addressing the challenges of domain-specific anomaly detection and comparison-based learning. Built upon a pretrained VLM backbone (<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, InternVL2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), SAGE consists of four main components: a visual encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with an Adapter for extracting image features, a text encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for processing textual inputs, a Fusion Attention layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{FA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{FA}</annotation></semantics></math> for capturing comparison embeddings between test and reference images, and an integrated LLM for multimodal reasoning and response generation, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ SAGE: A Visual Language Model for Anomaly Detection via Fact Enhancement and Entropy-aware Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In contrast to conventional VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, SAGE adopts a test-reference image paradigm, and we propose two fine-tuning strategies: the Self-Guided Fact Enhancement (SFE) and Entropy-aware Direct Preference Optimization (E-DPO) to adapt pretrained VLMs for anomaly detection and reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a test-reference image pair <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> where both <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t},I_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t},I_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, along with an input question <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of sequence length <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal is to fine-tune a VLM to generate a textual response that includes both an anomaly detection decision and an explanation. Specifically, the visual encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> adopts a Vision Transformer (ViT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to extract patch-level embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t},v_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{p^{2}\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t},v_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{p^{2}\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> from the test and reference images <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of patches and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. To enable comparison-based learning within the VLM, the input question <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is augmented with an auxiliary instructional prompt: “<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Above is the feature related to the anomaly facts</span>”. The combined input is then encoded by the text encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> into sequence embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{q}\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{q}\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, we employ the original pretrained VLM to generate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> textual fact sentences that describe potential anomalies and image patterns for each test image. This process is conducted offline and can be completed before fine-tuning the VLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During the SFE stage, for each test-reference image pair, we retrieve the corresponding generated fact sentences and encode them using the text encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the fact embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{F}\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{F}\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>. To enable comparison-based learning, the top-<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> patches are selected from the test image embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> based on their minimum cosine similarity to the reference image features <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming the comparison visual embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times\frac{H}{p}\times\frac{W}{p}\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times\frac{H}{p}\times\frac{W}{p}\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>. These embeddings are then used in the Fusion Attention layer <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{FA}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{FA}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a comparison-enhanced prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> via a cross-attention mechanism, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the query (Q), <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the key (K), and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the value (V). This Fusion Attention mechanism is employed to enhance the VLM’s awareness of test-reference discrepancies. Finally, the test image embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the question embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the comparison-enhanced prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated and fed into the LLM to generate the textual response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, during the E-DPO stage, the VLM is further refined to enhance its reasoning capabilities by leveraging multiple preference-ranked answers. The model is trained to adaptively align with expert preferences through entropy-aware optimization. E-DPO includes three steps: Score Calculation using an LLM, Answer Selection based on entropy differences, and model Optimization. Given a test-reference image pair <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding question <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we provide a set of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> rank answers <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},\cdots,a_{L_{N}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},\cdots,a_{L_{N}})</annotation></semantics></math> annotated by domain experts. We first perform the Score Calculation by leveraging an LLM (<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, GPT-4o) to compute Multiscale Logical Evaluation (MLE) scores for each ranked answer, serving as a proxy for answer quality. Then, the Answer Selection selects informative answer pairs based on their entropy differences. Finally, these selected pairs are used to fine-tune the policy model <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the Adapter in the visual encoder and LLM) through contrastive preference alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Self-Guided Fact Enhancement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although existing VLMs are capable of offering insightful reasoning for common anomalies, they often struggle to generalize to unseen categories or rare semantic anomalies. To address this limitation, we introduce the Self-Guided Fact Enhancement (SFE) module, which enables the VLM to better adapt to the anomaly detection domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a test-reference image pair <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, the pretrained VLM is used to generate a set of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> fact sentences <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\{s_{1},s_{2},\ldots,s_{L_{F}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\{s_{1},s_{2},\ldots,s_{L_{F}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that describe object-related characteristics, such as object category, potential anomaly types, and relevant semantic facts. These sentences are produced using our predefined prompts, for example, “<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What kind of anomalies does this type of object tend to exhibit?</span>” Each fact sentence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is then encoded by the text encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> into a fact embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming the fact embedding set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}=\{\mathbf{t}_{1},\mathbf{t}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{t}_{L_{F}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}=\{\mathbf{t}_{1},\mathbf{t}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{t}_{L_{F}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A global pooling operation is subsequently applied to each sentence embedding to obtain its corresponding pooled representation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a given test-reference image pair <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle I_{t},I_{r}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, the visual encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{v}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is used to extract patch-level embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> from the test and reference images, respectively. To identify the most anomalous regions, we compute the cosine similarity between each patch in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and all patches in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and select the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> patches in the test image that exhibit the minimum similarity to any reference patch. These selected features, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, are defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{d}=\underset{v_{i}\in v_{t}}{\text{argtop-$K$}}\left(\min_{v_{j}\in\mathbf{v}^{\prime}}\text{sim}(v_{i},v_{j})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>argtop-</mtext><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>sim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{d}=\underset{v_{i}\in v_{t}}{\text{argtop-$K$}}\left(\min_{v_{j}\in\mathbf{v}^{\prime}}\text{sim}(v_{i},v_{j})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(v_{i},v_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(v_{i},v_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cosine similarity between patch features <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{argtop-}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>argtop-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{argtop-}K</annotation></semantics></math> operator selects the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> patches in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the lowest similarity scores. This operation highlights regions in the test image that are most dissimilar to the reference, and thus more likely to exhibit anomalous patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, the Fusion Attention layer <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\text{FA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>FA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\text{FA}}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied to obtain the comparison-enhanced prompt <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> by integrating the test image embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the selected patch-level differences <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on the fact embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. This attention mechanism is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}=\phi_{\text{FA}}(t^{g},v_{t},v_{d})=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{t^{g}W_{q}(v_{d}W_{k})^{T}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)v_{t}W_{v}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>FA</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}=\phi_{\text{FA}}(t^{g},v_{t},v_{d})=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{t^{g}W_{q}(v_{d}W_{k})^{T}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)v_{t}W_{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the learnable projection matrices for queries, keys, and values, respectively. A multi-head attention mechanism is employed to enhance the interaction between fact sentences and visual patch embeddings. The attention operation is performed between each fact sentence and the corresponding comparison patches, and the outputs are stacked to yield the final comparison-enhanced embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the comparison-enhanced embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the test image embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the question embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated and fed into the LLM to generate the final answer. By leveraging fact priors and visual discrepancies, SFE effectively adapts the pretrained VLM to the anomaly detection domain, enhancing its interpretability and generalization to unseen cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Entropy-aware DPO</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limited anomaly-domain expertise in current VLMs and enhance their deeper reasoning capabilities in complex anomaly scenarios, we introduce an entropy-aware Direct Preference Optimization (E-DPO) strategy based on preference learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Considering the fact that human experts are able to recognize both normal and anomalous patterns while explaining root causes and downstream effects by drawing on prior experience and domain knowledge. Inspired by the instrumental fine-tuning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we hypothesize that anomaly reasoning preferences learning can be shared across diverse anomaly fields and encoded through unified learning. To support this optimization objective, we construct a preference-ranked anomaly reasoning dataset, AD-PL (described in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4. AD-PL ‣ SAGE: A Visual Language Model for Anomaly Detection via Fact Enhancement and Entropy-aware Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), which serves as supervision for fine-tuning the model toward expert-aligned reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">E-DPO consists of three steps: Score Calculation, Answer Selection, and Optimization. In the first step, Score Calculation, we leverage a large language model <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., GPT-4o) to evaluate each candidate answer <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> along three dimensions: defect type identification, defect localization, and logical reasoning for given question <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. These aspects are used to compute a unified Multiscale Logical Evaluation (MLE) score:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=\frac{1}{3}\left(\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{type}}(a_{i})+\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{loc}}(a_{i})+\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{logic}}(a_{i})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>type</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>loc</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>logic</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=\frac{1}{3}\left(\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{type}}(a_{i})+\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{loc}}(a_{i})+\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{logic}}(a_{i})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the overall score for the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th answer, and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{type}}(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>type</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{type}}(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{loc}}(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>loc</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{loc}}(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{logic}}(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>logic</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\text{LLM}}^{\text{logic}}(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> represent the individual evaluation scores for defect type recognition, localization precision, and reasoning quality, respectively. These scores provide fine-grained supervision signals for ranking and optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.07939v1/dataset222.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="A woman and a girl in white dresses sit in an open car.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An example from the AD-PL dataset. Each instance consists of a test-reference image pair and a corresponding question, accompanied by four preference-ranked answers (A1 to A4), with the responses becoming progressively less appropriate or increasingly erroneous from A1 to A4.</span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A woman and a girl in white dresses sit in an open car.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the entropy of these candidate answers to quantify the uncertainty and diversity among them. To this end, we normalize the MLE scores using a softmax function and calculate the entropy, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{i}=-\sum_{i=1}^{L_{N}}\text{softmax}(S_{i})\log\text{softmax}(S_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></munderover><mtext>softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mtext>softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}=-\sum_{i=1}^{L_{N}}\text{softmax}(S_{i})\log\text{softmax}(S_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy of the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th answer. This entropy reflects the confidence and informativeness of the model’s predictions. The lower entropy indicates more confident predictions, while higher entropy suggests ambiguity or conflicting signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, the Answer Selection step computes the most informative answer pairs based on entropy differences. Specifically, it selects the pair of adjacent ranked answers with the smallest entropy difference:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{i}=\arg\min_{1\leq i\leq L_{N}-1}\left|H(a_{i})-H(a_{i+1})\right|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{i}=\arg\min_{1\leq i\leq L_{N}-1}\left|H(a_{i})-H(a_{i+1})\right|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the index of the answer pair <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{i},a_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{i},a_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> that exhibits the minimal entropy difference, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta H_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta H_{min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the answer pair with the maximal entropy difference, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta H_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta H_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},a_{L_{N}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},a_{L_{N}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A large entropy difference reflects significant variation in answer quality, while a small entropy difference captures subtle distinctions, serving as fine-grained alignment.
To ensure the model learns both the differences and similarities between ranked answers, we incorporate a bias term into the Bradley–Terry framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite>. The modified objective is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x,a_{w},a_{l},\Delta H)=\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{w}|x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{l}|x)-\eta\Delta H," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x,a_{w},a_{l},\Delta H)=\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{w}|x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{l}|x)-\eta\Delta H,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta H\in\{\Delta H_{max},\Delta H_{min}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta H\in\{\Delta H_{max},\Delta H_{min}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy difference between the winning answers <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and the losing answers <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in the Optimization stage, E-DPO fine-tunes the policy model <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (including the Adapter and LLM) against the frozen model <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> (obtained after SFE fine-tuning) using an entropy-aware loss <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{E-DPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>E-DPO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{E-DPO}}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{EDPO}(\pi_{\theta})=-\mathbb{E}_{T}\left[\log\left(\frac{1}{1+\exp(-d(T))}\right)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{EDPO}(\pi_{\theta})=-\mathbb{E}_{T}\left[\log\left(\frac{1}{1+\exp(-d(T))}\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\{x,a_{w},a_{l},\Delta{H}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{x,a_{w},a_{l},\Delta{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta H_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta H_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages learning from distinguishable preference answers, while <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta H_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta H_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> refines the decision of ambiguous examples. <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to stabilize training via KL regularization. This formulation jointly enhances preference learning and alignment with expert preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Training and Inference</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training.</span> We adopt a two-stage training procedure. In the first stage (SFE), we train the adapter, the fusion attention layer, and the LLM to equip the model with domain knowledge of anomalies and comparison-based reasoning capabilities. In the second stage (E-DPO), the fusion attention layer is frozen, and further fine-tuning is applied to the adapter and LLM. The training objective for the LLM employs the standard cross-entropy loss, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\log(p_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ce</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\log(p_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tokens, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true label for token <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted probability. For E-DPO, we employ the maximum likelihood estimation as the training objective for a policy model parameterized by <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in Eq.  <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.3. Entropy-aware DPO ‣ 3. Method ‣ SAGE: A Visual Language Model for Anomaly Detection via Fact Enhancement and Entropy-aware Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference.</span> During the inference phase, the comparison-enhanced embedding obtained from the fusion attention layer is projected into a unified feature space via layer-normalized linear projections, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\text{input}}=\left[\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{v});\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{t}^{s});\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{t}^{q})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\text{input}}=\left[\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{v});\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{t}^{s});\phi_{Proj}(\mathbf{t}^{q})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{Proj}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{Proj}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a LayerNorm projection head. This unified representation is then fed into the LLM for auto-regressive generation following:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(Y_{a}|\mathcal{I},Y_{q})=\prod_{t=1}^{L}p_{\theta}(y_{t}|\mathcal{I},Y_{q},&lt;t,Y_{a},&lt;t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y_{a}|\mathcal{I},Y_{q})=\prod_{t=1}^{L}p_{\theta}(y_{t}|\mathcal{I},Y_{q},&lt;t,Y_{a},&lt;t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{a,&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{a,&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{q,&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{q,&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the answer and question tokens from all previous turns. Finally, the model decodes the generated token sequence <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{a}=[y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{a}=[y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> into a coherent natural language response.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of different methods on the MANTA and MPDD anomaly reasoning QA datasets under zero-shot and one-shot settings. Metrics include SBERT and accuracy across difficulty levels.</span></figcaption>
